# std/bash-tool's image (design/caos-expr.md, Phase 3): build this directory as
# a Rust project with rustc — the same shape as rgrep. `--src:@=.` hands rustc
# this tree (Cargo.toml + src/; rustc skips the caos metadata — .caos-expr,
# DEPS, DEEP-DEPS/), named by a LOCAL deep-deps mount (declared in DEPS) rather
# than /std, so std carries no ambient self-reference. No runner is named: rustc
# DEPENDS on the pool and curries the built binary onto it itself, so the result
# is curry(runner, worker1=<the compiled binary>) — a ready-to-run bounded bash
# tool in the shared runner pool.
#
# THE HELP IS BOUND HERE, so this tool describes itself. It was a hand-written
# `json!` declaration inside llm-step, which is also where `@writer` had to be
# known by matching the name "bash" -- and a copied description drifts (the
# same note std/merge/.caos-expr carries, for the same reason).
#
# `{array}` on `paths` is why this could not move sooner: every `@param` was a
# string, and declaring `paths` as one would have silently narrowed the tool
# from a list to a single value.
HELP=<<END
Run sh -c from the conversation root. Ordinary files (including memories and
skills) and source trees are writable together. Declare paths to read or edit
existing content; undeclared content stays lazy. Use mkdir, mv, cp -a and rm to
organize source trees. cp -a preserves their commit identity; editing their
files creates child commits when the result is stored. Conversation-root .caos
protocol metadata cannot be changed; a source tree's .caos/conflicts ledger is
editable.
@param cmd The command to run with sh -c.
@param {string} [cwd] Optional conversation-relative working directory; defaults to the conversation root.
@param {array} [paths] Conversation-relative files or directories to materialize, independent of cwd. A directory includes its descendants, including source trees.
@writer
@in
END
IMAGE=run --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/rustc --src:@=.
curry --base=$IMAGE --help=$HELP
